A **longer right whisker** suggests a tail stretching to the right and a **positive skew**. A median closer to the lower quartile can also support this description.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[thick] (0.5,1.2) -- (1.5,1.2);
\draw[thick] (3.2,1.2) -- (8.0,1.2);
\draw[thick] (0.5,0.95) -- (0.5,1.45);
\draw[thick] (8.0,0.95) -- (8.0,1.45);
\draw[thick,fill=orange!20] (1.5,0.7) rectangle (3.2,1.7);
\draw[thick] (2.2,0.7) -- (2.2,1.7);
\node[below] at (4.25,0.5) {positively skewed};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A **longer left whisker** suggests a tail stretching to the left and a **negative skew**. A median closer to the upper quartile can also support this description.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[thick] (0.5,1.2) -- (5.3,1.2);
\draw[thick] (7.0,1.2) -- (8.0,1.2);
\draw[thick] (0.5,0.95) -- (0.5,1.45);
\draw[thick] (8.0,0.95) -- (8.0,1.45);
\draw[thick,fill=orange!20] (5.3,0.7) rectangle (7.0,1.7);
\draw[thick] (6.3,0.7) -- (6.3,1.7);
\node[below] at (4.25,0.5) {negatively skewed};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

If the **whiskers are roughly equal** and the median sits near the centre of the box, the distribution is **approximately symmetrical**.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
